//! Logger library for Rust applications
//!
//! This package provides utilities for configuring and managing structured logging in Rust applications.
//!
//! This package offers two approaches to logger configuration:
//!   1. Programmatic Logger struct
//!   2. Proto-based LoggerConfig
//!
//! Both approaches ultimately configure and install a global tracing subscriber instance.
//!
//! # Key Features
//!
//!   - Multiple output formats (JSON for production, Console for development)
//!   - Configurable log levels (Trace, Debug, Info, Warn, Error)
//!   - Environment variable-based configuration
//!   - Development and production modes
//!   - Pretty-printing option for JSON logs
//!   - Delayed logging for messages before logger initialization
//!   - Trace level optimization checks
//!
//! # Basic Usage - Logger
//!
//! Using the Logger struct:
//!
//! ```no_run
//! use logger::{Logger, LogFormat, LogLevel};
//!
//! let mut logger = Logger {
//!     format: LogFormat::Json,
//!     level: LogLevel::Info,
//!     development: false,
//!     ..Default::default()
//! };
//! logger.install().expect("Failed to install logger");
//! tracing::info!("Application started");
//! ```
//!
//! Loading from environment variables:
//!
//! ```no_run
//! use logger::Logger;
//!
//! let mut logger = Logger::default();
//! logger.load_from_env().expect("Failed to load logger config");
//! logger.install().expect("Failed to install logger");
//! ```
//!
//! # Basic Usage - Proto Logger
//!
//! Using the proto-based LoggerConfig:
//!
//! ```no_run
//! use logger::install_from_config;
//! use types::sys::logger::types::{LoggerConfig, Format, Level};
//!
//! let config = LoggerConfig {
//!     format: Format::Json.into(),
//!     level: Level::Info.into(),
//!     development: false,
//!     output_paths: vec!["stdout".to_string()],
//!     ..Default::default()
//! };
//! install_from_config(&config).expect("Failed to install logger");
//! ```
//!
//! # Environment Variables
//!
//! The package reads configuration from the following environment variables:
//!   - LogLevel: Log level (trace, debug, info, warn, error)
//!   - LogFormat: Log format (json, console)
//!
//! Example:
//!
//! ```bash
//! export LogLevel=debug
//! export LogFormat=console
//! ```
//!
//! # Log Levels
//!
//! Log levels in order of increasing severity:
//!   - Trace: Most verbose, detailed debugging
//!   - Debug: Debugging information
//!   - Info: Informational messages about normal operations
//!   - Warn: Warning messages about potentially problematic situations
//!   - Error: Error messages about failures and problems

